# Docker Commands: Pull, Run & Manage Containers

---

## The Core Workflow

Every time you work with Docker, you follow the same fundamental flow:

```
Docker Hub (Registry)
        │
        │  docker pull
        ▼
  Docker Image (on your machine)
        │
        │  docker run
        ▼
  Docker Container (running environment)
        │
        │  docker stop
        ▼
  Container Stopped (image still exists)
```

---

## Essential Commands Reference

### Checking Your Installation
```bash
docker -v
# Returns: Docker version 20.10.x — confirms Docker is installed and working
```

---

### Pulling an Image from Docker Hub
```bash
docker pull docker/getting-started
```
- Downloads the image from Docker Hub to your local machine
- You'll see **multiple layers downloading** — this is the layered image system in action
- Each layer is a separate dependency, pulled and cached independently

> **Key point:** `docker pull` only downloads. It does NOT run anything yet.

---

### Listing Downloaded Images
```bash
docker images
```
Output looks like:
```
REPOSITORY              TAG       IMAGE ID       SIZE
docker/getting-started  latest    abc123def456   28.8MB
hello-world             latest    feb5d9fea6a5   13.3kB
```

---

### Running an Image as a Container
```bash
docker run -d -p 80:80 docker/getting-started
```

Breaking down every flag:

| Flag | Full Meaning | What It Does |
|---|---|---|
| `docker run` | — | Tells Docker to create and start a container from an image |
| `-d` | Detached mode | Runs the container in the background — frees your terminal |
| `-p 80:80` | Port mapping | Maps host port 80 → container port 80 |
| `docker/getting-started` | Image name | The image to use |

If the image isn't found locally, `docker run` **automatically pulls it first** — so `docker pull` is optional if you're going to run anyway.

