## Port Mapping — The Most Important Concept Here

This confuses many beginners. Here is the clean mental model:

```
Your Host Machine (Windows/Mac/Linux)
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                         │
│   localhost:80  ──────────────────────┐ │
│                                       │ │
│   ┌─── Container Environment ───────┐ │ │
│   │                                 │ │ │
│   │   App running on port 80  ◄─────┘ │ │
│   │                                   │ │
│   └───────────────────────────────────┘ │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The container is an **isolated environment** — it has its own internal network. To reach the app running inside it, you must create a **bridge** between a port on your host and a port inside the container.

```bash
-p HOST_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT
-p 80:80       # host port 80 talks to container port 80
-p 5000:80     # host port 5000 talks to container port 80
-p 8080:3000   # host port 8080 talks to container port 3000
```

### Running Multiple Containers — Port Rules

You can run as many containers as you want simultaneously, but **host ports must be unique**. Container ports can repeat freely.

```
Host Machine
├── localhost:80    ──►  Container A (app on port 80)
├── localhost:5000  ──►  Container B (app on port 80)   ✅ valid
└── localhost:8080  ──►  Container C (app on port 3000) ✅ valid

# You CANNOT do:
├── localhost:80    ──►  Container A
└── localhost:80    ──►  Container B   ❌ port conflict on host
```

