### Checking Running Containers
```bash
docker ps
```
Shows only **currently running** containers with their IDs, image names, ports, and uptime.

```bash
docker ps -a
```
Shows **all containers** — including stopped ones. Useful for debugging.

---

### Stopping a Container
```bash
docker stop <container_id>
```
- Gracefully stops the running container
- The **image is untouched** — it still exists on your machine
- You can restart it or run a fresh container from the same image anytime

---

### Removing an Image
```bash
docker image remove <image_id>

# If it errors due to linked container history, force it:
docker image remove -f <image_id>
```

> **Why the error?** Even after stopping a container, Docker keeps metadata linking it to the image. You either remove the container first (`docker rm <container_id>`) or force-remove the image.

